static.solution:
{e}^{2 x+3 y}={e}^{2 x}\cdot {e}^{3 y}  to compute the double integral as the product of two single integrals. This gives



\begin{array}{rcl}
\int\!\!\int _{\mathcal{R}} e^{2 x + 3 y}\,dA\amp =\amp 
\int_0^{8}\int_0^{4}{e}^{2 x}{e}^{3 y} \,dx \,dy\\
\amp =\amp \left(\int_0^{4}{e}^{2 x} \,dx\right)\left(\int_0^{8}{e}^{3 y} \,dy\right)\\
\amp =\amp \left(\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2 x}\bigg|_0^{4}\right)\left(\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3 y}\bigg|_0^{8}\right)\\
\amp =\amp \frac{1}{2}\left({e}^{8}-{e}^0\right)\cdot \frac{1}{3}\left({e}^{24}-{e}^0\right)\\
\amp =\amp \frac{1}{6}\left({e}^{8}-1\right)\left({e}^{24}-1\right)
\approx 1.31561\times 10^{13}
\end{array}
